Here's the complete project. It builds and runs successfully:

**Program.cs** — wires up HTTP transport with `MapMcp`:

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol;
using McpHttpServer;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithHttpTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly()
    .WithResources<ConfigResourceProvider>();

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapMcp();

app.Run();
```

**Tools/EchoTool.cs** — example tool class:

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public static class EchoTool
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Echoes the input message back to the caller.")]
    public static string Echo([Description("The message to echo")] string message)
        => $"Echo: {message}";

    [McpServerTool, Description("Returns the current server time.")]
    public static string GetServerTime()
        => DateTime.UtcNow.ToString("O");
}
```

**ConfigResourceProvider.cs** — exposes resources via `[McpServerResource]`:

```csharp
[McpServerResourceType]
public class ConfigResourceProvider
{
    [McpServerResource(UriTemplate = "config://app/settings",
        Name = "App Settings", MimeType = "application/json")]
    public static string GetAppSettings() { ... }

    [McpServerResource(UriTemplate = "config://app/features",
        Name = "Feature Flags", MimeType = "application/json")]
    public static string GetFeatureFlags() { ... }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `AddMcpServer().WithHttpTransport()` configures HTTP/SSE transport
- `app.MapMcp()` maps the MCP endpoint (defaults to `/mcp`)
- `[McpServerResourceType]` marks a class for resource discovery
- `[McpServerResource]` exposes methods as MCP resources with a URI template
- Clients connect via `http://localhost:<port>/mcp` using Streamable HTTP

